---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import PassportPhoto from '../islands/PassportPhoto';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Which passport photo size should I choose?', a: '35×45 mm and 2×2 inch (51×51 mm) are the most common sizes worldwide, and 33×48 mm is used by some visa offices. Requirements differ by country and change over time, so always confirm the size on the official website.' },
  { q: 'How do I print passport photos at home or a print shop?', a: 'Download the <strong>4×6″ print sheet</strong> and print it as a standard 4×6 inch (10×15 cm) photo at “actual size” / 100%, not “fit to page”. Then cut along the light gray lines.' },
  { q: 'Can I make a photo under 20KB or 50KB for an online form?', a: 'Yes. Choose the exact pixel size the form asks for (use Custom if needed) and pick a max file size. PDFSherpa keeps the dimensions and lowers JPG quality until the file fits.' },
  { q: 'Does it change my background to white?', a: 'Not automatically. For the best results take the photo in front of a plain white or light gray wall with even light, so no editing is needed.' },
  { q: 'Is my photo uploaded?', a: 'No. Cropping, resizing and the print sheet are all created in your browser. Your photo never leaves your device.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="passport-photo-maker"
  title="Passport Photo Maker – 35×45, 2×2 & Print Sheet | PDFSherpa"
  description="Make passport and visa photos at 35×45 mm, 2×2 inch or custom pixel sizes, with a KB limit for online forms and a 4×6 print sheet. Free, no upload."
  h1="Passport Photo Maker"
  lead="Turn any photo into a passport or visa photo: crop with a face guide, get the exact size at 300 DPI, keep it under a KB limit for online forms, and print copies on a 4×6 sheet."
  howToTitle="How to Make a Passport Photo"
  steps={['Choose a size and select your photo.', 'Drag the box so your face fits the oval guide.', 'Click <strong>Create</strong>, then download the photo or the 4×6 print sheet.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['resize-image', 'crop-image', 'compress-image', 'jpg-to-pdf']}
>
  <PassportPhoto client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What the Passport Photo Maker does</h2>
    <p>
      It crops your photo to the exact proportions of a passport or visa photo, scales it to the right pixel size at 300 DPI, and saves a JPG you can
      upload to online applications. It can also lay out several copies on a standard 4×6 inch photo print, so you can print them for a few rupees or cents
      instead of paying a studio.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Pick a size:</strong> 35×45 mm (413×531 px), 2×2 inch (600×600 px), 33×48 mm, or Custom pixels if a form specifies them.</li>
      <li><strong>Select your photo.</strong> Phone photos are rotated correctly automatically.</li>
      <li><strong>Position your face.</strong> Move and resize the box until your face sits inside the dashed oval, with a little space above your head.</li>
      <li><strong>Set a file size limit</strong> if an online form needs one, for example 50KB.</li>
      <li><strong>Create and download</strong> the single photo for forms, or the 4×6″ sheet for printing.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Tips for an accepted photo</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Stand about a metre in front of a plain white or light gray wall, with no shadows behind you.</li>
      <li>Face the camera straight on with a neutral expression, eyes open, and no hair covering your face.</li>
      <li>Use daylight from a window in front of you, not the phone flash.</li>
      <li>Ask someone else to take the photo from chest height, not a selfie at arm’s length, which distorts faces.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For form uploads, see <a href="/guides/photo-signature-size-for-online-applications">photo and signature size rules for online applications</a>.
      Rules vary between countries and portals, so check the official requirements every time.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      PDFSherpa doesn’t detect faces, replace backgrounds or check compliance automatically. It gives you precise sizing and printing, while the photo
      itself must meet the authority’s rules. Some offices only accept photos from approved studios.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>Your face is personal data. PDFSherpa processes the photo entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded, stored or used for anything else.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
